**Theorem 2.2 (equal original coordinates, different canonical histories).**
Under Assumption 1.2, the cuts of Definition 2.1 satisfy

$$
n(x_0)=n(x_1),\qquad O^+(x_0)=O^+(x_1),\qquad x_0\ne x_1.
$$

More strongly, their raw records differ only in the two symbol fields
on70 at $c+7,c+8$, which are respectively $01$ and $10$. For each
$V\in\{S,B,M_J,T\}$ separately, $V(x_0)\ne V(x_1)$.

Proof. The gate, mode, header and length are identical. RCA Definition
13.2, relocated by ARC Definition 5.3 and preserved by GCA Definition
3.1, gives each positive payload iteration as

$$
\mathrm{RD}(\text{length},5);\ \mathrm{IN}(6);\ \mathrm{EX}(70);
\ \mathrm{WR}(70,6);\ \mathrm{MR}(70);\ \mathrm{MR}(\text{length}).
$$

It tests the already supplied length, not the payload bit. No bank
assignment, payload import or payload-dependent branch occurs inside
this loop. Both lengths are three. The first two iterations therefore
have identical row/PC, allocation, cursor, token and receipt schedules.
Their differing inputs affect only latch6 and its matching70 writes.
Their third positive length test is also the same. Its common IN0
overwrites latch6 with0 before the chosen cuts. Latch5 contains the
same length-test result; every other latch is preserved. Thus all
finite slots agree, all heads agree, all tapes other than the stated
two70 symbols agree, and the incomplete frame has the same extent
and seal status.

TMC PR57.D1 appends an ordinary receipt for these rows, not a transcript
of their operands. The differing IN/WR values therefore do not change
the meter symbols, its extent or either meter head. This verifies the
meter equality as well as equality of its count. The external supplier
returns to its common ready state after each bit, as in AIF Definition
2.2; its future choices are not an endpoint coordinate. The two histories
differ at their first payload IN and remain different histories. They
are acquired canonical pre-outer00 prefixes by GENERAL Definition 1.4.
Apply its Corollary 4.4 separately to the four views. ∎

